Apple's rumored 14-inch and 16-inch MacBook Pro models with touch-enabled OLED displays are currently being tested with macOS 27.1, which is scheduled for release in late October, according to the latest word <a href="https://www.bloomberg.com/news/articles/2026-09-14/upcoming-apple-products-what-to-expect-in-late-2026-and-early-2027">from <em>Bloomberg</em>'s Mark Gurman</a>.<br/>
<br/>
<img src="https://images.macrumors.com/article-new/2024/10/Apple-MacBook-Pro-M4-hero.jpg" alt="" width="3840" height="2160" class="aligncenter size-full wp-image-974025" /><br/>
While he previously said these high-end MacBook Pro models would be released in <a href="https://www.macrumors.com/2026/04/19/when-to-expect-next-macbook-pro-and-mac-studio/">either late 2026 or early 2027</a>, Gurman's latest report today has the laptops firmly within a list of products coming "later this year," so it would appear that he is now more confident that Apple is planning to release them before the end of 2026.<br/>
<br/>
Other rumored features for these MacBook Pro models include <a href="https://www.macrumors.com/2026/06/26/oled-macbook-stick-with-m5-pro-and-m5-max/">M5 Pro and M5 Max chips</a>, a thinner design, and a Dynamic Island instead of a notch.<br/>
<br/>
<em>Macworld</em> previously reported that these laptops <a href="https://www.macrumors.com/2026/04/27/apple-planning-to-launch-two-new-ultra-products/">would have MacBook Ultra branding</a>, but that same report incorrectly said the iPhone Duo would be named the iPhone Ultra. In any case, the first-generation MacBook Pro models with OLED displays are expected to be distinct, higher-priced models that sit above the regular 14-inch and 16-inch MacBook Pro models, which are expected to <a href="https://www.macrumors.com/2026/07/12/why-apple-is-skipping-m6-pro-m6-max-chips/">jump to the M7 Pro and M7 Max chips</a> next year.<br/>
<br/>
